A native type whose host spelling is a pointer has no composite literal.

`#[go.native(pkg, "*time.Ticker")] struct Ticker {}` names a Go type this
language never declares -- the same thing `#[cnative("*C.cairo_t")]` does for
cairo, one scheme over. That form is what lets fyne's and gtk4's `time.timer`
hold the schedule `Every` handed back instead of an integer index into a
registry the runtime kept, which was the whole reason those registries existed.

A program cannot build a value of such a type, so the only literal that reaches
the emitter is the empty one the checker synthesizes for an uninitialised
`var t Ticker`. Rendered as a composite literal it came out `*time.Ticker{}`,
which does not parse: a pointer type has no `{}` form. The zero of a pointer is
nil.

The var is assigned and called on, because the two halves travel separately:
`method` puts the receiver before the dot, and the mark's name is what the
field is declared as.

deny out/go/fyne/model.go `\*time\.Ticker\{\}` -- a pointer type has no composite literal
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `(?m)^\tt__inst\d+ +Ticker$` -- the field is the Go type the mark names, not the SNGL name

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import go "sngl:language/go"

#[go.native("time", "*time.Ticker")]
struct Ticker {}

#[go.native("time", "time.NewTicker")]
func newTicker(d int) Ticker

#[go.native("time", "time.Ticker.Stop", method)]
func Ticker.stop()

output {
    go {
        fyne
    }
}

component main ui.node {
    var t Ticker

    ui.button(text="arm", @click { t = newTicker(5) })
    ui.button(text="stop", @click { t.stop() })
}

ui.window(title="zero") { main }
-- out/go/fyne/model.go --
